 data center, allowing clients to test AI applications at scale before committing to wider deployments. Dubbed AI Factory Munich, the cluster lets customers assess AI workloads against real-world infrastructure, security, data, governance, and operational requirements.\n\nNTT Europe CEO Anne-Sophie Lotgering said Nvidia and Dell supplied the infrastructure, while Red Hat and Mistral AI supported the platform and model. The cluster uses Nvidia Blackwell GPUs connected via Spectrum-X Ethernet, with Dell providing servers and storage.\n\n“Every leader I speak to wants AI they own, control and trust. At NTT Data's AI factory Munich, they can run their own applications and data in a production data center, on their terms, before they commit to deployment. It‘s the difference between a slide and a decision,” said Graham Clarke, managing director, global private and sovereign AI, NTT Data.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSO-TFG: A Configurable Seed-Free On-the-Fly Twiddle Factor Generator for NWC-Based NTT/INTT | IEEE Journals & Magazine | IEEE Xplore (https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11172676/)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; # CSO-TFG: A Configurable Seed-Free On-the-Fly Twiddle Factor Generator for NWC-Based NTT/INTT ... The number theoretic transform (NTT) simplifies complex polynomial multiplications into element-wise inner products, and is therefore, widely adopted in various hardware ... ... For polynomial rings, negative wrapped convolution (NWC) is often used within the NTT to avoid additional zero-padding and degree expansion.\n\n# CSO-TFG: A Configurable Seed-Free On-the-Fly Twiddle Factor Generator for NWC-Based NTT/INTT\n\nPublisher: IEEE\n\n  * Download PDF\n  * Download References\n  * Request Permissions\n  * Save to\n  * Alerts\n\n\nCSO-TFG: A Configurable Seed-free On-the-fly Twiddle Factor Generator for NWC-Based NTT/INTT\n\n## Abstract:\n\nThe number theoretic transform (NTT) simplifies complex polynomial multiplications into element-wise inner products, and is therefore, widely adopted in various hardware ...Show More\n\n## Metadata\n\n## Abstract:\n\nThe number theoretic transform (NTT) simplifies complex polynomial multiplications into element-wise inner products, and is therefore, widely adopted in various hardware accelerators. For polynomial rings, negative wrapped convolution (NWC) is often used within the NTT to avoid additional zero-padding and degree expansion. However, NWC-based NTT uses nearly twice as many twiddle factors (TFs) as the standard NTT, significantly increasing memory demands for constrained applications like fully homomorphic encryption (FHE). Existing hardware designs primarily focus on accelerating butterfly computations in the NTT, while ignoring the memory bottleneck caused by TFs. In this letter, a configurable seed-free on-the-fly TF generator (CSO-TFG) is proposed, which dynamically generates all required TFs without using any seeds and supports flexible degree configurations. The CSO-TFG is deployed on a Xilinx ZYNQ-7 FPGA, and experimental results demonstrate that it achieves a 18%–56% improvement in throughput-per-area (TPA) compared to state-of-the-art works.\n\nCSO-TFG: A Configurable Seed-free On-the-fly Twiddle Factor Generator for NWC-Based NTT/INTT\n\nPublished in: IEEE Embedded Systems Letters ( Volume: 18, Issue: 3, June 2026)\n\nPage(s): 173 - 177\n\nDate of Publication: 18 September 2025\n\nISSN Information:\n\nDOI: 10.1109/LES.2025.3610521\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNTT | 4-Step Algorithm (https://zprize.hardcaml.com/ntt-4step.html)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3.9 years ago; The idea is to break the large transform into lots of smaller transforms. ... The 4-step algorithm performs $2 . 2^12$ smaller NTT transforms, each of size $2^12$. ... However, we must add to this a further $2 . 2^24$ operations to perform the scaling operation (the scaling requires 2 multiplications—one to scale the coefficient, the other to update the scaling factor). ... Fortunately as we scale up the design for performance we can read multiple columns at a time.\n\n# 4-Step Algorithm\n\nThe core design limits us to transform sizes which can fit within on-chip FPGA RAM resources. To break this limit we use the well known 4-step algorithm as described in this paper. An OCaml reference implementation is provided in our repository.\n\nThe idea is to break the large transform into lots of smaller transforms. Let’s consider our target transform size of $2^24$. We first reformulate the input data as a $2^12 x 2^12$ matrix in row major order.\n\nThe following steps are then performed:\n\n  1. Perform $2^12$ NTTs on each column.\n  2. Multiply each element of the matrix at location $(i,j)$ by $ω^(i.j)$, where $ω$ is the appropriate root of unity of the transform size.\n  3. Perform $2^12$ NTTs on each row.\n  4. Transpose the result.\n\nThe required NTT transform can now easily fit within on-chip FPGA RAM resources, so long as we can store the complete matrix in external memory and access it efficiently.\n\n## Performance\n\nUsing the Cooley-Tukey FFT algorithm, a transform of size $2^24$ requires $2^24 log_{2} 2^24 = 402,653,184$ operations.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSAM: A Scalable Accelerator for Number Theoretic Transform Using Multi-Dimensional Decomposition (https://people.iiis.tsinghua.edu.cn/~gaomy/pubs/sam.iccad23.pdf)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Crawled: 2.9 years ago; power-of-2 value that cannot be factorized in the RNS way. ... Later, they also implemented an NTT co-processor [9] that used two parallel butterfly units, but limited to N = 2^12. ... Kim et al. [21] ported various GPU optimizations that originally targeted DFT to accelerate NTT. ... Previous NTT hardware designs [16]–[18]