$ p $$$; $$$ {\\hat{a}}_j $$$, $$$ {a}_i $$$ and the twiddle factor $$$ {\\psi_N}^{ij} $$$ are all defined over a finite field of bit-width $$$ \\lambda $$$. ... First, we can expand the input vector $$$ \\mathbf{a} $$$ to a length of the nearest power of 2 by padding with zeros. ... Finally, $$$ {\\hat{a}}_j $$$ is the sum-up of $$$ {\\hat{a}}_j^{+} $$$ and $$$ {\\hat{a}}_j^{-} $$$. ... In case of huge input size, an NTT task can be decomposed once into multiple sub-tasks. ... Therefore, we use the prior partition method to decompose a large-size NTT task into plenty of smaller sub-tasks that can be processed on an on-chip core.\nFirst, we can expand the input vector $$$ \\mathbf{a} $$$ to a length of the nearest power of 2 by padding with zeros. Next, the NTT task can be divided into two sub-tasks based on odd and even subscripts of $$$ {\\hat{a}}_j $$$:\n\n$$$$ {\\hat{a}}_j^{+}\\left({\\psi_N}^j\\right)={a}_0+{a}_2\\cdotp {\\psi_N}^j+\\cdots +{a}_{N-2}\\cdotp {\\psi_N}^{\\left(\\frac{N}{2}-1\\right)j} $$$$ (3)\n\n$$$$ {\\hat{a}}_j^{-}\\left({\\psi_N}^j\\right)={a}_1+{a}_3\\cdotp {\\psi_N}^j+\\cdots +{a}_{N-1}\\cdotp {\\psi_N}^{\\left(\\frac{N}{2}-1\\right)j} $$$$ (4)\n\n$$$$ {\\hat{a}}_j\\left({\\psi_N}^j\\right)={\\hat{a}}_j^{+}\\left({\\psi_N}^{2j}\\right)+{\\psi_N}^j\\cdotp {\\hat{a}}_j^{-}\\left({\\psi_N}^{2j}\\right) $$$$ (5)\n\nIn this way, the $$$ {\\psi}_{\\frac{N}{2}} $$$ is used to generate the $$$ \\frac{N}{2} $$$ twiddle factors, which are further used in calculating $$$ {\\hat{a}}_j^{+} $$$ and $$$ {\\hat{a}}_j^{-} $$$. Finally, $$$ {\\hat{a}}_j $$$ is the sum-up of $$$ {\\hat{a}}_j^{+} $$$ and $$$ {\\hat{a}}_j^{-} $$$. According to (5), the time complexity of reducing the sub-tasks is $$$ O(n) $$$. And the time complexity of the recursive implementation of NTT based on divide-and-conquer is as follows:\n\n$$$$ T(n)=2T\\left(n/2\\right)+O(n)=O\\left(n\\cdotp \\log n\\right) $$$$ (6)\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------